1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:03,018
Welcome to MIST.

2
00:00:03,018 --> 00:00:08,873
This is a live walkthrough of the application, using a completely fictional service request.

3
00:00:08,873 --> 00:00:21,220
We will follow that request from the Customer, through each human routing and delivery decision, into independent quality control, and finally back to the Customer as an assured service product.

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:26,383
Every journey starts at the secure sign-in screen.

5
00:00:26,383 --> 00:00:32,678
The workspace shown after authentication is shaped by the person’s role and authorised scope.

6
00:00:32,678 --> 00:00:36,603
Navigation is useful, but it is not the security boundary.

7
00:00:36,603 --> 00:00:43,710
MIST also checks permission, assignment and access to the individual request whenever an action is taken.

8
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:54,124
The Customer creates a structured request, describing the need, the operational context, the expected service product and the desired delivery date.

9
00:00:54,124 --> 00:01:02,570
Clear fields reduce avoidable back-and-forth, while the draft option lets the Customer prepare the request before formally submitting it.

10
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:07,974
Once submitted, the request remains visible in the Customer’s own register.

11
00:01:07,974 --> 00:01:16,198
The detail page brings together status, current ownership, important dates, conversation history and the developing product.

12
00:01:16,198 --> 00:01:22,300
This means the Customer can follow progress without needing access to internal staff queues.

13
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,872
The JIOC Routing User performs the first named routing decision.

14
00:01:27,872 --> 00:01:34,164
They review the original request, confirm the appropriate route and record the reason for that decision.

15
00:01:34,164 --> 00:01:44,570
At this point, and at later authorised stages, the request can be returned to the Customer for more information instead of being pushed forward with an unclear requirement.

16
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:19,869
The Request Coordination User, represented here by the DGIOC stage, reviews the routed request and coordinates the next organisational destination.

17
00:02:19,869 --> 00:02:21,950
The decision remains human-led.

18
00:02:21,950 --> 00:02:30,880
MIST records who acted, what changed and why, while keeping the Customer’s original requirement attached to the same service journey.

19
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:22,899
At NCGI-A Ops, the Ops Routing User selects the accountable delivery team.

20
00:03:22,899 --> 00:03:32,366
Organisational siblings can be chosen as genuine destinations, and the selected route determines which authorised team queue receives the work.

21
00:03:32,366 --> 00:03:38,590
MIST supports the decision with context, but it does not choose the team on the user’s behalf.

22
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,189
The OSG Team Manager receives the request in the team queue, reviews demand and assigns an appropriate Team Analyst.

23
00:04:37,189 --> 00:04:47,230
Assignment is explicit and attributable, making it clear who owns the work while allowing the Manager to balance workload, skill and availability across the team.

24
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:03,923
The Team Workspace is the team’s shared operating picture.

25
00:05:03,923 --> 00:05:10,215
Its work queue, board and calendar help staff see priorities, due dates and work in progress.

26
00:05:10,215 --> 00:05:21,310
People, statistics and activity views support workload awareness and service insight, while notices and pinned links keep useful team information close to the work.

27
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:27,460
Each user also has a profile and personal working tools.

28
00:05:27,460 --> 00:05:33,022
The profile shows their authorised role, team membership and relevant working information.

29
00:05:33,022 --> 00:05:46,370
My assigned actions provides a focused list of work that needs that person’s attention, and the personal calendar brings together due dates and appointments without exposing private appointment details to colleagues.

30
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:54,835
The OSG Team Analyst claims the assigned item and develops the service product.

31
00:05:54,835 --> 00:06:07,810
The request context, acceptance expectations and conversation stay connected to the task, so the Analyst can work from one governed record rather than assembling the story from separate messages and spreadsheets.

32
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:21,562
If a material detail is missing, the Analyst can send a formal clarification request back to the Customer.

33
00:06:21,562 --> 00:06:28,991
MIST pauses the relevant workflow task, records the question and presents an authorised response action to the Customer.

34
00:06:28,991 --> 00:06:35,740
When the Customer replies, the same Analyst journey resumes with the answer preserved in the request history.

35
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:42,122
When the product is ready, the Analyst first chooses how it will be supplied.

36
00:08:42,122 --> 00:08:50,372
Upload product to MIST opens the managed file route, where supported documents and images are stored, scanned and validated.

37
00:08:50,372 --> 00:08:54,430
Add a product link opens only the approved HTTPS link fields.

38
00:08:54,430 --> 00:09:00,949
The two routes are intentionally separate, so the page asks for only the information that applies.

39
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:08,113
After the product has been added, one clear Submit product action creates the exact review version and its checksum.

40
00:09:08,113 --> 00:09:11,057
The covering note is released with that version.

41
00:09:11,057 --> 00:09:20,870
If any content changes later, MIST creates a new version, protecting the connection between what the Analyst submitted and what each reviewer actually approved.

42
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:37,809
The work returns to the OSG Team Manager for product review.

43
00:09:37,809 --> 00:09:47,205
The Manager can inspect the product contents, author, covering note and checksum before approving the exact package for independent quality control.

44
00:09:47,205 --> 00:09:54,570
A rejection would return the work for correction, with the reason recorded rather than lost in an informal exchange.

45
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:11,028
The QC Manager’s shared queue provides oversight of incoming assurance work.

46
00:11:11,028 --> 00:11:16,982
It shows the request and its current review state without collapsing separation of duties.

47
00:11:16,982 --> 00:11:24,790
The QC Manager can monitor flow, while the independent QC User takes responsibility for the detailed quality decision.

48
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:33,686
The QC User claims the review, opens the same Manager-approved product package and records an independent decision.

49
00:11:33,686 --> 00:11:38,364
The immutable version and checksum make the review target unambiguous.

50
00:11:38,364 --> 00:11:44,380
MIST keeps the evidence, decision, actor and time attached to the request’s audit history.

51
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:55,797
After QC approval, the workflow returns to the QC Manager for dissemination.

52
00:12:55,797 --> 00:13:04,318
The Manager confirms the Customer can access the product, reviews the release details and deliberately chooses Disseminate to Customer.

53
00:13:04,318 --> 00:13:10,630
This final human decision releases the assured package and covering note to the originating request.

54
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:28,572
The Customer now sees Product available on the original request.

55
00:14:28,572 --> 00:14:34,288
They can open the released information, accept the product and provide feedback.

56
00:14:34,288 --> 00:14:45,433
From first submission to final acceptance, MIST keeps one visible, attributable chain of routing, assignment, clarification, production, review and release.

57
00:14:45,433 --> 00:14:49,220
That is the MIST story: request, coordinate, deliver.
